.Sh DESCRIPTION
These methods perform a key derivation according to PKCS#12 (RFC7292)
with an input password
.Fa pass
of length
.Fa passlen ,
a salt
.Fa salt
of length
.Fa saltlen ,
an iteration count
.Fa iter
and a digest algorithm
.Fa md_type .
The ID byte
.Fa id
determines how the resulting key is intended to be used:
.Bl -bullet
.It
If ID=1, then the pseudorandom bits being produced are to be used as key
material for performing encryption or decryption.
.It
If ID=2, then the pseudorandom bits being produced are to be used as an
IV (Initial Value) for encryption or decryption.
.It
If ID=3, then the pseudorandom bits being produced are to be used as an
integrity key for MACing.
.El
.Pp
The intended format of the supplied password is determined by the method
chosen:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Fn PKCS12_key_gen_asc
and
.Fn PKCS12_key_gen_asc_ex
expect an ASCII-formatted password.
.It
.Fn PKCS12_key_gen_uni
and
.Fn PKCS12_key_gen_uni_ex
expect a Unicode-formatted password.
.It
.Fn PKCS12_key_gen_utf8
and
.Fn PKCS12_key_gen_utf8_ex
expect a UTF-8 encoded password.
.El
.Pp
.Fa pass
is the password used in the derivation of length
.Fa passlen .
.Fa pass
is an optional parameter and can be NULL. If
.Fa passlen
is -1, then the function will calculate the length of
.Fa pass
using
.Xr strlen 3 .
.Pp
.Fa salt
is the salt used in the derivation of length
.Fa saltlen .
If the
.Fa salt
is NULL, then
.Fa saltlen
must be 0. The function will not attempt to calculate the length of the
.Fa salt
because it is not assumed to be NULL terminated.
.Pp
.Fa iter
is the iteration count and its value should be greater than or equal to
1. RFC 2898 suggests an iteration count of at least 1000. Any
.Fa iter
less than 1 is treated as a single iteration.
.Pp
.Em digest
is the message digest function used in the derivation.
.Pp
The derived key will be written to
.Fa out .
The size of the
.Fa out
buffer is specified via
.Fa n .
.Pp
Functions ending in
.Xr _ex 3
allow for a library context
.Fa ctx
and property query
.Fa propq
to be used to select algorithm implementations.
.Sh NOTES
A typical application of this function is to derive keying material for
an encryption algorithm from a password in the
.Fa pass ,
a salt in
.Fa salt ,
and an iteration count.
.Pp
Increasing the
.Fa iter
parameter slows down the algorithm which makes it harder for an attacker
to perform a brute force attack using a large number of candidate
passwords.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Returns 1 on success or 0 on error.
.Sh CONFORMING TO
IETF RFC 7292
.Pf ( Lk https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc7292 )
